  Visual Screenshot Comparison – How to Use
This framework supports visual validation by comparing screenshots against a previously captured expected (baseline) image.
The goal is to:
Capture an expected screenshot once
Reuse it in future runs to validate the UI visually
Store actual screenshots only during the test run (not permanently)

1️⃣ Capturing an Expected (Baseline) Screenshot (One-Time)
Use this step only once when you want to create the expected image.
Feature file example:
Then capture screenshot baseline with the name "cis/expected/homepage.png"
What this does:
Takes a screenshot at that point in the test
Saves it to:
src/test/resources/visual-baselines/cis/expected/homepage.png
This file becomes the expected reference for future comparisons
✅ After running this once, commit the image to Git
❗ You may remove this step from the feature later if you don’t need it anymore

2️⃣ Validating the Page Against the Expected Screenshot
Use this step during normal test runs to compare the current UI with the saved baseline.
Feature file example:
Then the page should match baseline "cis/expected/homepage.png"

What this does:
Captures an actual screenshot during the test run
Compares it with the expected image
If they match → test continues normally
If they do NOT match:
Saves:
target/visual-results/actual/homepage.png
target/visual-results/diff/homepage_diff.png
Fails the test with a clear error message
📌 Actual screenshots are not reused and are cleaned on the next run.

3️⃣ When Should I Use Visual Comparison?
Recommended use cases:
Important pages (Home, Dashboard, Summary screens)
Layout-sensitive UIs
Regression checks after UI changes
Not recommended for:
Highly dynamic pages (timestamps, live data)
Pages with frequently changing content unless stabilized
  
